ent_names/322"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Pommelled Horse, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "9 – 17 August 2008"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/832"} "Guojia Tiyuguan, Beijing Aolinpike Gongyuan, Beijing"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/259979"} "76 from 27 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The format was the same as it had been from 1984-2004, with eight gymnasts advancing from the team all-around, but no more than two per nation. China’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/107235"} "Xiao Qin"] " had won the last three World Championships, was known as the “Pommel King,” and was expected to win. He led the qualifying with 16.000 points, light-years ahead of the other gymnasts. His final performance had a slight break in routine but he still won the gold medal with 15.875 points. Silver and bronze medals went to Croatian " [:a {:href "/athletes/111963"} "Filip Ude"] " and Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/111967"} "Louis Smith"] ", respectively, although this was only decided after a tie-breaker. Ude’s medal was the first ever in Olympic gymnastics for Croatia, while Smith won Britain’s first individual gymnastic medal since " [:a {:href "/athletes/30181"} "Walter Tysall"] " in 1908. He would return in 2012 at London and add an Olympic silver medal on pommels."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "124"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/107235"} "Xiao Qin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CHN.png"}] "CHN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr